Recorded output from the programs the Net module reads.

WHERE THESE CAME FROM, exactly, because a fixture whose provenance is unknown is
a fixture that tests whoever wrote it:

    2026-08-27, from `os7img:116` — the shipped amd64 image, IMAGE_VERSION
    1.0.0.116 — run in a container on an x64 Windows host through Docker
    Desktop. Each file is the verbatim stdout of the command named below.

    ip-addr.json             ip -j addr show
    ip-route4.json           ip -j route show
    ip-route6-empty.json     ip -j -6 route show
    rfkill-unavailable.json  rfkill -J        (EXIT CODE 1 — see below)

The two `dig-srv-*.json` files came from somewhere else, on the same day and the
same host: a REAL Active Directory domain controller — Samba 4.23.6, realm
`OS7.TEST`, the container `installer/testing/Dockerfile.ad-dc` builds — with
`dig` from bind9-dnsutils inside it. The verbatim command line is in each file.

    dig-srv-ldap.json        dig +nocmd +noall +comments +answer @127.0.0.1 \
                               SRV _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.os7.test
    dig-srv-none.json        dig +nocmd +noall +comments +answer @127.0.0.1 \
                               SRV _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.nosuchdomain.os7.test

THESE TWO ARE AN ENVELOPE, NOT RAW STDOUT, and the difference is deliberate.
`{ command, exitcode, stdout, stderr }` — because dig's exit code and the
emptiness of its stderr are both part of what is being recorded, and the file
that could not carry an exit code is the paragraph at the bottom of this README.
The `command` field is in the file so that a fixture and its provenance cannot
drift apart while this README is edited by hand.

It has a second effect worth naming, because it was not the reason and is the
larger one. This directory is NOT in the `-text` list in `.gitattributes` — the
Zfs and Directory fixtures are, and Net's have never been — so `* text=auto
eol=lf` decides these files' line endings on every checkout. An envelope keeps
the recorded bytes inside JSON strings as `\n` and `\t`, where no end-of-line
filter can reach them. A raw dig capture in this directory would be a
measurement an unrelated setting is allowed to edit.

FOUR OF THE FIVE RECORDS IN `dig-srv-ldap.json` WERE PLANTED, and saying so is
the point of this section. A one-DC lab answers with one SRV record, and a sort
that has only ever seen one record has not been tested (BUILD-NOTES #89), so
`dc2`, `dc3`, `dc5` and `branch-dc` were added to the DC's own DNS with
`samba-tool dns add` before the capture, at priorities 0, 5, 100 and 10 and
weights 20, 100, 0 and 50, and they were added in an order that is NOT the order
they must come back in. The DC then answered for real: the format, the column
padding, the TTLs and the record order on the wire are Samba's, not anybody's
idea of what Samba would say.

WHAT THEY COVER, AND WHAT THEY DO NOT.

A container's network namespace is Docker's, so these carry a loopback and one
veth. That is enough for the shapes the parsers actually branch on, and the
self-test says which branch each case exercises:

  * a link with two addresses in one `addr_info` (lo: 127.0.0.1 and ::1)
  * a link with one (eth0)
  * a default route, which HAS a `gateway` and has NO `prefsrc`
  * a kernel route, which has `prefsrc` and NO `gateway` — the case that
    catches a cmdlet written on a machine that happened to have both, because
    `ip -j` OMITS keys rather than emitting nulls and Set-StrictMode turns
    reaching for a missing one into a terminating error
  * an empty table (`[]`), which must read as "no routes" and not as an error

The dig pair covers a multi-record answer and the answer that means "there are
none", which are two different facts that print almost the same:

  * FIVE SRV records, arriving in the order dc1 (0/100), dc5 (100/0), dc2
    (0/20), branch-dc (10/50), dc3 (5/100) — priority/weight — which is not the
    order they must come back in. The numbers are chosen so that a STRING sort
    and a NUMERIC one disagree in BOTH fields: "10" sorts before "5", and "20"
    sorts before "100" when the heavier weight has to win. A two-record fixture
    with priorities 0 and 1 would have caught neither.
  * one record on port 3268 rather than 389, so a parser that hardcoded the
    LDAP port fails here instead of passing
  * NXDOMAIN, which is an ANSWER — the name has no records — and which dig
    reports with EXIT CODE 0 and no output at all under `+short`

Three more cases live in the self-test rather than here, and each is labelled
where it sits:

  * A SINGLE-record answer, verbatim from `SRV _ldap._tcp.pdc._msdcs.os7.test`
    on the same DC in the same session — the PDC record, which a domain has
    exactly one of. It is inline because it also carries the other spelling of
    an answer line: that owner name is long enough that dig separates it from
    the TTL with a TAB, where the shorter name in `dig-srv-ldap.json` gets
    SPACES.
  * NOTHING ANSWERING: `+time=1 +tries=1 @127.0.0.99`, which exits **9** and
    writes `;; no servers could be reached` to STDOUT, not stderr — measured,
    and the reason `Resolve-NetSrvRecord` reads stdout for its Reason.
  * SERVFAIL WITH EXIT 0, which is the case an exit-code reader gets wrong.
    The header text in the self-test is written out rather than captured, but
    the claim under it is not: a twenty-line UDP responder answering one query
    with RCODE 2 and one with RCODE 5 established that a real dig exits **0**
    for both SERVFAIL and REFUSED and puts the status in the `+comments`
    header and nowhere else.

WHAT IS STILL OWED, and must come from real hardware rather than being written
by hand here:

  * A WIRELESS link. `ip -j` cannot be used to recognise one — a Wi-Fi adapter
    reports `link_type: "ether"` exactly like a wired one (measured) — so the
    module asks sysfs for `phy80211`. Only the NEGATIVE case is recorded; the
    self-test builds a sysfs tree of its own for the positive one and labels it
    as constructed rather than measured.
  * `rfkill -J` WITH A RADIO IN IT. This repository has never seen that output,
    which is why `Get-NetRadio` passes its `Devices` through raw instead of
    typing fields nobody has measured.
  * A link with NO carrier, and one that is administratively down.
  * A machine with more than one default route.
  * SRV records from a WINDOWS SERVER domain controller. Samba answers the DNS
    protocol, so the wire format is real, but the record set a real forest
    publishes — site-aware `_ldap._tcp.<site>._sites.dc._msdcs`, `_gc._tcp`,
    the `DomainDnsZones` names — has never been seen here. Nothing in the
    parser depends on which names exist; what is untested is whether a domain
    of that shape answers in a form this parser has not met.
  * A SERVFAIL from a REAL resolver failing for a real reason. dig's behaviour
    on one is measured (exit 0, status in the header); what a recursive
    resolver that has genuinely lost its upstream prints alongside it is not,
    and if it ever prints something this parser reads as an answer, nothing
    here would notice.

`rfkill-unavailable.json` IS THE INTERESTING ONE. `rfkill -J` exits **1** when
/dev/rfkill is absent and still prints a syntactically valid, EMPTY device list.
A caller reading the JSON alone concludes "no radio is blocked" about a machine
that cannot be asked at all — so the self-test replays this file twice, once
with exit 1 and once with exit 0, and requires `Get-NetRadio` to answer
differently. The file records the output; the exit code lives in the test beside
it, because a `.json` file cannot carry one.
